2. Compare Spring and Spring boot? Benefits of spring boot?
Spring is the core framework, spring boot is a toolkit built on top of it.
Spring needs to manually add configuration, set up server, add dependencies. While spring boot is less manual
work, because this framework does the boilerplate for us. The benefits of spring boot, first, it is beginner
friendly because of the auto configuration. It has embedded server like Tomcat. There are many annotations we
can directly use instead of manual configuration.

3. What is IOC and what is DI?
IOC is inversion of control. Instead of manually creating java objects, we give the control to spring boot, so
it helps us manage beans. DI is dependency injection. It provides dependency from outside, but not creating them
inside the object.

4. What's @ComponentScan?
It tells the spring to auto scan our beans and manage the bean lifecycle for us.

5. What is @SpringBootApplication
It is made from 3 annotations, @Configuration, @EnableAutoConfiguration, @ComponentScan. We put this annotation
on top of the app class. It manage all beans inside the folder.

6. How many ways to define a bean?
a. Constructor, the most useful and efficient one. It protect the bean that can't be overwritten and good for test.
@Autowired
public Constructor(Service service) {
    this.service = service;
}
b. Setter, if the dependency is optional
@Autowired
public void set(Service service) {
    this.service = service;
}
c. field, the least use, hard to test, not good for security
@Autowired
private Service service

7. What's the default bean name of @Component and @Bean? Compare @Component and @Bean?
@Component default name is the lowercase version of class name.
@Bean default name is the name of method
@Bean is for method level, when we want to use code developed by third party since we can't directly use their
class. We can use @Autowired to do dependency injection so We have full control over the method.
@Component is for class level, for classes that created by our own. Spring boot auto scans it as a bean.

8. Compare @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller
@Controller, where we define the api, how we get requests and return responses.
@Service, defines business logics
@Repository, interact with database and process the data
@Component, mark a class as a bean, let spring boot auto manage the bean for us

9. @Autowired, @Qualifier, @Resource, @Primary
@Autowired defines where we want to do dependency injection
@Qualifier, clarify which bean we want to inject
Example: @Qualifier("beanName")
@Resource: Another way to do injection, put it on top of method name, @Resource(name="beanName")
@Primary: Mark the bean as primary, when multiple beans handle same tasks, spring boot use the primary first
Qualifier > primary

